00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.459 align:start position:0%
 
welcome back well we've heard from the  politicians now it's time to hear from  the voice of business I carry on  Fairburn the head of the CBI and joins  us now thank you very much for being  with us so well you were last on the  show in November so do you feel more  confident or less confident about brexit  now  oh well Sophie I think we really are in  the emergency zone of brexit now so I  think last November we have the  possibility of a deal on the table and  that has now that feels that has receded  we don't have a deal on the table  firms are having to plan in the here and  now for a No Deal brexit with all of the  pain that that will bring we know that  businesses are leaving the country we  know that businesses are making plans  that will damage communities across the  country and just this week we had a new  chapter in the unfolding nightmare which  is the prospect that the trade deals  that the the UK businesses enjoy through  the European Union will not be ready in  time for leaving so South Korea in  particular a real concern for businesses  so it really is the emergency zone on  those trade deals your deputy attended a  meeting with Olympics about the  possibility of the existing trade deals  that we enjoy as being part of the EU  being rolled over what I mean what is  the position as far as you understand  well I think ministers and officials  have been working very hard so it's not  for ones of trying  but it's genuinely hard because partner  countries who we've enjoyed free trade  deals with now for a number of years  under the EU are actually saying what we  want to see what your relationship is  first Japan is saying that for example  South Korea one of our main trading  partners they want to reopen terms so  the prospect is that the day after  brexit those deals will disappear and  what does that mean good jobs for the  court what does it mean for normal  people well I think that the the really  important thing about why what business  says matters is that this is how brexit  would be felt in communities across the  country it will be the decisions that  businesses take about jobs and  investment and they will reduce them so  you have fewer potential to potential to  trade globally that will mean less  investment in the future and that will  mean fewer jobs in the future we've seen  Sony move its headquarters I'm talking  to businesses that are planning for  price increases  for job reductions after a no job brexit  this is very very real now now you were  saying about when we last week there was  at least the prospect of a deal and you  feel that that is now receding what do  you think and the chances of a No Deal  well they feel as though they have gone  up because the possibility of the Prime  Minister's deal passing feels as that  has receded and I think what business is  saying is compromise find that middle  ground and at the moment it feels like  the parliamentary process is in logjam  no way can be found through so that  prospect of No Deal feels much higher  but what I think businesses everyone I  speak to is saying get round the table  find that compromise and get that deal  so we can take I mean hope the  compromise be the Jeremy Corbyn proposal  the permanent customs union well there  are number of things when that letter  was published I think businesses will  have looked at that and thought yep  there is no number of things here that  could work for business we at the CBI  have said that actually a customs union  of some sort would be a good thing for  most businesses but it's not the only  solution so with that on the table now  from from from labor we would really  urge politicians and leaders from all  parties to get run that around the table  and find a solution on that there isn't  just one model that won't work for  business that would work but there are  others so you basically take any deal is  better than no deal is that what you're  saying the things that matter for  business are frictionless trade access  for our services and access to people  and talent any deal that delivers along  those lines will work for business the  issue now is time and we are heading  towards that cliff edge so we need that  deal so you think you're talking about  the issue it's time less than 50 days to  go until brexit so what our business is  doing right now well I think there's  been real acceleration in the planning  so we have got stockpiling we know that  warehousing capacity has run out there  is they are preparing for disruption at  borders but I think that the bigger  thing that's going on is that there is  also a recalibration if you like of what  UK is like as a place to invest we have  been a fantastic country to invest in  for many years a gateway to Europe we  have great timezone great universities  and yet um investors are looking at the  UK and asking questions about what we  like for the long term hence the  decision by Nissan BRICS it wasn't the  only factor but it was one  so you having conversations with this  long-term telling you that that they  were not going to invest or that they  are going to move us well absolutely so  these plans are on the stocks and they  are still traveling in hope around a  deal so some have taken action but  others are waiting many small businesses  have not been able to take action at all  so they will hit be hit by the shock but  the point is that we we have this  opportunity now to get a deal on the  table pass it and move on  and return the UK to being its it the  great place to invest that it has been  but this isn't this is real danger time  now it's interesting hearing you talk do  you when you look at what's happening in  Westminster what do you feel towards  politicians I think that there is just  an astonishment that businesses feel  that can turn into anger actually  because we have a political process here  that is not delivering a solution for  our country so here we are ten years on  from the financial crash we have we we  had a risk here we're going to recreate  some of the conditions around the  financial crash the Bank of England  today has a forecast which puts growth  at the lowest it has been in the last  ten years why would we voluntarily do  that to ourselves as a country we know  that the financial crash harms the  youngest in our population most young  people into the workplace why would we  do that again and I think there there is  a sense that this is almost negligence  on behalf of our political institutions  or political leaders in not delivering a  solution to this we have 47 days left  that solution needs to be found if it  breaks it that that's the problem  or the way that it's been handled I  think brexit the decision that was taken  52 48 was always going to be challenging  however here we are 47 days to go again  that number and we actually are not only  further on if you're a business in terms  of having clarity about what the trading  environment will be when we leave and  that that has the process has not  delivered any clarity at all so we need  that to happen very fast now okay clear  message thank you very much being  program

